<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bar'an Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arsh Bilqis, Throne of Bilqis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bar'an Temple, known locally as Arsh Bilqis ('Throne of Bilqis'), is a Sabaean temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaqah">Almaqah</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Built and enlarged over several centuries from the early first millennium BCE, it is known for its row of tall stone pillars. It was excavated by the German Archaeological Institute in the late twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baran-temple.jpg" alt="Bar'an Temple" width="320" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ali Mohamed · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barran_Temple_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
